gatehouse binds inside the 'internet' namespace, e.g.:
  ip netns exec internet /home/hewn/mosschat/mosschat gatehouse --bind 203.0.113.1:443 --secondary-bind 203.0.113.1:444 --community <hex> --members <path>

house-a and house-b reach it as 203.0.113.1:443 / :444 through their own NAT.

== confirm the NAT mode with conntrack, never assume it ==
Only one address exists past nat-a's outward side (203.0.113.1, the
internet bridge), so the probe below varies the DESTINATION PORT, not
the destination address, and it must be sent from one FIXED local
source port -- two different ephemeral source ports would get two
different mappings under EITHER mode and prove nothing. See
README.md 'Confirming which NAT mode is really in effect' for the
exact socat commands, then run:

  ip netns exec nat-a conntrack -L -n -s 10.1.0.2

Interpretation: each line shows two tuples, the original request and
the reply tuple as the NAT sees it. The mapped external port is the
reply tuple's dport= field (e.g. '... src=203.0.113.1 dst=203.0.113.11
sport=443 dport=41234 ...', src/dst/sport/dport in that order, same as
README.md's worked example), not the sport on the request side. If the
two probes (dest port 443, then 444) show the SAME reply dport=, the
NAT is endpoint-independent (EIM). If they show two DIFFERENT reply
dport= values, it is endpoint-dependent, i.e. symmetric (EDM).
Do this for nat-b with -s 10.2.0.2 too.

